Chapter: DiagCad
  cites Works chapter GopUsbKbd
  cites Diag chapter DiagStage

 A USB HID boot keyboard report (HID 1.11 appendix B) is eight bytes: byte 0
 the modifier bits (bit 0 left Ctrl, 2 left Alt, 4 right Ctrl, 6 right Alt),
 byte 1 reserved, bytes 2 to 7 the usages held down. Usage 0x4C (76) is
 Delete and 0x63 (99) keypad Delete (HID Usage Tables, page 07).

 0xCF9 (3321) is the chipset reset control register: writing 2 then 6 sets
 SYS_RST and then RST_CPU, a hard reset. Port 0x64 (100) command 0xFE (254)
 pulses the 8042's reset line on boards that still carry one; a controller
 that is absent reads 0xFF (255) and is not written.

 We say:

Section: The Chord

  dcad-ctrl : Integer = 17
  dcad-alt : Integer = 68

  dcad-has-del : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  dcad-has-del (rep) (i) =
    if i >= 8 then False
    else let u = peek-byte rep i
    in if u == 76 | u == 99 then True
    else dcad-has-del rep (i + 1)

  dcad-hit : UsbKbd -> Boolean
  dcad-hit (k) =
    if k.uk-ok == False then False
    else let m = peek-byte (k.uk-report) 0
    in if bit-and m dcad-ctrl == 0 then False
    else if bit-and m dcad-alt == 0 then False
    else dcad-has-del (k.uk-report) 2

Section: The Reset

  dcad-spin : Integer -> Integer
  dcad-spin (n) = dcad-spin (n + 1)

  dcad-reset : Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  dcad-reset (n) = act
    a <- port-out-byte 3321 2
    b <- port-out-byte 3321 6
    s <- port-in-byte 100
    p <- if s /= 255 then port-out-byte 100 254 else 0
    dcad-spin 0
  end

Section: The Poll

 Four pumps per poll: `kbd-pump-one` advances one phase (arm, check, drain),
 so one poll can arm the endpoint, take the report and test it.

  dcad-pumps : UsbKbd, Integer -> Boolean
  dcad-pumps (k) (n) =
    if n <= 0 then False
    else let p = kbd-pump-one k
    in if dcad-hit k then True
    else dcad-pumps k (n - 1)

  dcad-poll : DiagCtx -> [Device.Port] Integer
  dcad-poll (c) = act
    if dcad-pumps (c.dc-kbd) 4 then dcad-reset 0 else 0
  end
